# Git Workflow Rules

You are a Git expert. Follow these conventions for clean, traceable history.

## Commit Message Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <short description>

[optional body]
[optional footer]
```

### Types
| Type | Use For |
|------|---------|
| `feat` | New feature |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature |
| `style` | Formatting, whitespace (no code change) |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `test` | Adding or updating tests |
| `chore` | Build, dependencies, tooling |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |

### Examples
```bash
feat(scheduler): add drag-and-drop for class reordering
fix(api): handle null branch_id in schedule query
refactor(auth): extract session validation to hook
docs(readme): add setup instructions for local Supabase
chore(deps): update @tanstack/react-query to 5.x
test(forms): add validation tests for schedule form
```

### Scope (Optional but Helpful)
Use the feature area or component name:
- `scheduler`, `maintenance`, `reports`
- `api`, `auth`, `db`
- `ui`, `forms`, `modal`

## Branch Naming

```
<type>/<short-description>
```

### Branch Types
| Prefix | Use For |
|--------|---------|
| `feature/` | New features |
| `fix/` | Bug fixes |
| `refactor/` | Code refactoring |
| `chore/` | Maintenance tasks |
| `hotfix/` | Urgent production fixes |

### Examples
```bash
feature/hierarchy-report
fix/session-deduplication
refactor/query-key-factory
chore/update-dependencies
hotfix/auth-redirect-loop
```

### Rules
- Use lowercase with hyphens
- Keep it short but descriptive
- Include ticket number if applicable: `feature/ABC-123-user-profile`

## Atomic Commits

### One Logical Change Per Commit
```bash
# ❌ Bad: Multiple unrelated changes
git commit -m "fix login bug, add new header, update deps"

# ✅ Good: Separate commits
git commit -m "fix(auth): handle expired token redirect"
git commit -m "feat(ui): add responsive header component"
git commit -m "chore(deps): update react to 18.3"
```

### Commit Frequently
- Commit after each logical step
- Makes reverting easier
- Provides better history for debugging

## What to Commit

### Always Commit
- Source code changes
- Configuration files (`.env.example`, `tsconfig.json`)
- Migration files (but see Supabase rules below)
- Package.json / lock files
- Documentation

### Never Commit
```gitignore
# Environment files with secrets
.env
.env.local
.env.production

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build outputs
.next/
dist/
build/

# IDE settings (personal)
.idea/
.vscode/settings.json

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Database dumps (large, contain data)
*.dump
*.backup
*.sql.gz

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
```

## Supabase-Specific Rules

### Migration Files
```bash
# ✅ Commit new migration files
git add supabase/migrations/20240901120000_add_users_table.sql
git commit -m "feat(db): add users table with RLS policies"

# ⚠️ NEVER modify an already-applied migration
# If migration is in production, create a NEW migration instead

# ❌ Don't commit these
supabase/.temp/        # Internal state
supabase/.branches/    # Branch state
```

### Before Pushing Migration Changes
```bash
# Verify migration status
supabase migration list

# Check for uncommitted schema changes
supabase db diff
```

## Pull Request Guidelines

### PR Title
Follow commit message format:
```
feat(scheduler): implement drag-and-drop class ordering
```

### PR Description Template
```markdown
## Summary
Brief description of changes

## Changes
- Added X component
- Fixed Y bug
- Updated Z logic

## Testing
- [ ] Unit tests pass
- [ ] Manual testing completed
- [ ] Tested on mobile viewport

## Screenshots (if UI change)
[Attach screenshots]

## Migration Notes (if applicable)
- New migration: `20240901_add_column.sql`
- Requires `supabase db push`
```

### PR Size
- Aim for <400 lines changed
- Split large features into smaller PRs
- Each PR should be reviewable in <30 minutes

## Common Git Operations

### Undo Last Commit (Keep Changes)
```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

### Amend Last Commit
```bash
git add .
git commit --amend --no-edit
```

### Stash Changes
```bash
git stash                    # Stash changes
git stash pop                # Apply and remove stash
git stash list               # View stashes
git stash drop stash@{0}     # Delete specific stash
```

### Rebase onto Main
```bash
git checkout feature/my-branch
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main

# If conflicts, resolve then:
git add .
git rebase --continue
```

### Cherry-Pick Commit
```bash
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
```

## Pre-Commit Checklist

Before committing, verify:
- [ ] Code compiles without errors
- [ ] Linting passes (`npm run lint`)
- [ ] Tests pass (`npm run test`)
- [ ] No console.log statements left
- [ ] No commented-out code
- [ ] Environment variables not hardcoded
- [ ] Sensitive data not included

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

```bash
# ❌ Don't commit everything at once
git add .
git commit -m "changes"

# ✅ Stage specific files, write descriptive message
git add src/components/Schedule.tsx
git commit -m "fix(scheduler): prevent duplicate class times"

# ❌ Don't use generic messages
git commit -m "fix"
git commit -m "update"
git commit -m "WIP"

# ✅ Be specific
git commit -m "fix(api): return 404 for non-existent schedule"

# ❌ Don't force push to shared branches
git push --force origin main  # DANGEROUS!

# ✅ Force push only to your own feature branches
git push --force-with-lease origin feature/my-branch

# ❌ Don't commit generated files
git add types/supabase.ts  # Generated by CLI

# ✅ Regenerate on build or add to .gitignore
```

## Useful Aliases

Add to `~/.gitconfig`:
```ini
[alias]
  co = checkout
  br = branch
  ci = commit
  st = status
  last = log -1 HEAD
  unstage = reset HEAD --
  graph = log --oneline --graph --all
  amend = commit --amend --no-edit
```
